<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Demo DocLang document, and the viewer's DocLang fixture.

  DocLang (doclang.ai) is what a document conversion writes: the structure
  a converter found in a PDF or a scan, in a vocabulary an LLM can read.
  This file is shaped like that output, a two-page memo with running
  headers and footers, a paragraph and a list split by the page break, a
  footnote, a table with merged cells, a chart with its data, a code
  listing, a formula, and a form. Open it with ?url=sample.dclg.
-->
<doclang xmlns="https://www.doclang.ai/ns/v0" version="0.7">
  <head>
    <default_resolution width="512" height="512"/>
  </head>

  <page_header>
    <layer value="furniture"/>
    <location value="40"/><location value="20"/><location value="470"/><location value="30"/>
    Excelano · Records Programme · Internal
  </page_header>

  <heading>
    <location value="40"/><location value="50"/><location value="470"/><location value="70"/>
    Reading a Converted Document
  </heading>
  <text>
    <location value="40"/><location value="78"/><location value="470"/><location value="86"/>
    A memo on what the converter kept, written as the converter would write it
  </text>

  <heading level="2">Summary</heading>
  <text>
    <thread thread_id="1"/>
    When a scanned report is converted, the result is not the report. It is a
    record of what the converter <bold>recognised</bold>: which lines were
    headings, which blocks were paragraphs, where a table began and ended, and
    what the cells said. This memo is written in that form so that the viewer
    can be judged on the same material a real conversion produces. Its
    <italic>shape</italic> matters more than its subject, so the subject is
    the memo itself. The paragraph you are reading was
    <text><href uri="https://www.doclang.ai/"/>written in DocLang</text>,
    and it continues on the next page, where the viewer joins it back into
    one paragraph.
  </text>

  <heading level="2">What the converter keeps</heading>
  <text>
    Structure survives conversion better than looks do. The list below is
    ordered<marker><xref thread_id="7"/><superscript>1</superscript></marker>
    because the source numbered it:
  </text>
  <list class="ordered">
    <thread thread_id="2"/>
    <ldiv><marker>1.</marker></ldiv>
    <text>Headings, with their level, which is what makes an outline possible.</text>
    <ldiv><marker>2.</marker></ldiv>
    <text>
      Paragraphs, with inline <underline>emphasis</underline>, the occasional
      <strikethrough>correction</strikethrough>, subscripts like H<subscript>2</subscript>O,
      and a <handwriting>margin note</handwriting> in someone's hand.
    </text>
    <ldiv><marker>3.</marker></ldiv>
    <text>Lists, including the ones that check things off:</text>
    <list>
      <ldiv><marker><checkbox class="selected"/></marker></ldiv>
      <text>Confirm page count</text>
      <ldiv><marker><checkbox/></marker></ldiv>
      <text>Confirm every table has a caption</text>
    </list>
  </list>

  <footnote>
    <thread thread_id="7"/>
    <superscript>1</superscript> The converter records the printed number as
    the item's marker, so a renumbered list still shows the numbers the page
    showed.
  </footnote>

  <page_footer>
    <layer value="furniture"/>
    Page 1 of 2
  </page_footer>
  <page_break/>

  <page_header>
    <layer value="furniture"/>
    Excelano · Records Programme · Internal
  </page_header>

  <text>
    <thread thread_id="1"/>
    That is the point of a thread: a component the page split is one
    component to the reader.
  </text>

  <list class="ordered">
    <thread thread_id="2"/>
    <ldiv><marker>4.</marker></ldiv>
    <text>Tables, as the grid the converter saw, merged cells included.</text>
    <ldiv><marker>5.</marker></ldiv>
    <text>Pictures, and for a chart, the numbers behind it.</text>
  </list>

  <heading level="2">Tables</heading>
  <table>
    <caption>Table 1. Conversion quality on the sample set, by document type</caption>
    <corn/>Document type<ched/>Pages<ched/>Recognised<lcel/><nl/>
    <ucel/><ucel/><ched/>Headings<ched/>Tables<nl/>
    <rhed/>Scanned reports<fcel/>412<fcel/>97%<fcel/>91%<nl/>
    <rhed/>Born-digital PDFs<fcel/>1,180<fcel/>99%<fcel/>98%<nl/>
    <rhed/>Forms<fcel/>96<fcel/>88%<fcel/>—<nl/>
    <srow/>All document types<lcel/><lcel/><lcel/><nl/>
    <rhed/>Total<fcel/>1,688<fcel/>98%<fcel/>96%<nl/>
  </table>
  <text>
    A row header stays on the left, a column header stays on top, and the
    corner cell is the one where both meet. The merged header shows how the
    converter records a cell that spans two columns.
  </text>

  <heading level="2">Pictures and charts</heading>
  <picture class="chart">
    <label value="bar_chart"/>
    <caption>Figure 1. Documents converted per quarter</caption>
    <description>A bar chart of four quarters, rising from 160 to 340.</description>
    <src uri="data:image/svg+xml;base64,"/>
    <tabular>
      <ched/>Quarter<ched/>Documents<nl/>
      <fcel/>Q1<fcel/>160<nl/>
      <fcel/>Q2<fcel/>220<nl/>
      <fcel/>Q3<fcel/>300<nl/>
      <fcel/>Q4<fcel/>340<nl/>
    </tabular>
  </picture>
  <picture>
    <label value="photograph"/>
    <caption>Figure 2. The scanning station</caption>
    <description>A flatbed scanner on a desk, with a stack of bound reports beside it.</description>
    <text>SCAN STATION 2</text>
    <text>Reports in → Records out</text>
  </picture>
  <text>
    A picture is its image. When the archive that held the image is not
    beside the file, the viewer shows what the converter read off it
    instead, which is what Figure 2 is doing above.
  </text>

  <heading level="2">Code and formulas</heading>
  <code>
    <label value="Shell"/>
    <caption>Listing 1. Converting one report</caption>
    <content>docling report.pdf --to doclang
axe/view/?url=report.dclg</content>
  </code>
  <text>
    The recognition rate in Table 1 is the share of true components the
    converter found, <formula>r = \frac{\text{found}}{\text{present}}</formula>,
    and the figure the programme tracks is its complement:
  </text>
  <formula>
    <caption>Equation 1. Miss rate</caption>
    m = 1 - \frac{\text{found}}{\text{present}}
  </formula>

  <heading level="2">Forms</heading>
  <text>
    A form converts to a field region: each item is a key with its values,
    and a value the form left blank stays blank.
  </text>
  <field_region>
    <field_heading>Conversion request</field_heading>
    <field_item>
      <key>Requested by:</key>
      <value>Records Programme</value>
    </field_item>
    <field_item>
      <key>Source:</key>
      <value>Bound reports, 1998–2004</value>
      <value>Loose correspondence, 2005</value>
    </field_item>
    <field_item>
      <key>Target date:</key>
      <value class="fillable"></value>
      <hint>Format: YYYY-MM-DD</hint>
    </field_item>
    <field_item>
      <key>Priority:</key>
      <value><checkbox class="selected"/> Normal</value>
      <value><checkbox/> Urgent</value>
    </field_item>
  </field_region>

  <group>
    <caption>Sign-off</caption>
    <text>Reviewed against the source pages by <handwriting>D. Anderson</handwriting>.</text>
  </group>

  <page_footer>
    <layer value="furniture"/>
    Page 2 of 2
  </page_footer>
</doclang>
